allatum, into his council.","why":"Hammurabi governed through a small circle of men bound to him personally.","people":[]},{"n":3,"lv":3,"band":"religious","name":"Priests and cloistered women","loc":"Šangûm","term":"bārûm (diviner), nadītum","lbl":"c. 0.5–1% (est.)","w":0.75,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/old-babylonia-3.jpg","you":"Your family dedicated you to the sun god, and you live in the walled cloister at Sippar; you may not marry, but you lease out your fields and lend silver in your own name.","kid":"Priests cared for the temples of the gods, and some women, called nadītu, lived together in a walled cloister.","rise":"Priestly offices passed within families or were bought; a girl became a nadītu when her family dedicated her and guaranteed her keep, and what she owned went back to her family when she died.","why":"Each city’s great temple was the household of its god, run by a chief administrator (šangûm) and served by priests of many kinds: some fed and clothed the god’s statue, others sang laments to calm the gods, and…","people":[]},{"n":4,"lv":4,"band":"specialists","name":"Scribes","loc":"Ṭupšarrum","term":"","lbl":"c. 0.2–0.5% (est.)","w":0.35,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/old-babylonia-4.jpg","you":"You spent your boyhood copying signs, word lists and Sumerian proverbs in your teacher’s house; now you write the contracts, letters and receipts of the palace, the temples and the merchants.","kid":"Scribes wrote letters, contracts and laws by pressing wedge-shaped marks into soft clay.","rise":"Writing opened the way into the king’s service: Hammurabi’s secretaries handled state secrets, and one of them, Sin-iddinam, became governor of Larsa.","why":"Scribes wrote the contracts, letters, receipts and court records on which Babylonian life ran, pressing wedge-shaped signs into clay.","people":[]},{"n":5,"lv":5,"band":"specialists","name":"Merchants","loc":"Tamkārum","term":"kārum (the merchants' body)","lbl":"c. 0.5–1% (est.)","w":0.75,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/old-babylonia-5.jpg","you":"You lend silver and grain at interest, send agents out with your capital and buy copper shipped from Dilmun; the Code limits the interest you may charge.","kid":"Merchants bought and sold goods such as copper, and lent silver and grain to people who needed them.","rise":"Trading and lending were open to anyone with silver, and temples and nadītu lent at interest too; credit could fail, and part of Ea-nasir’s house was later joined to a neighbour’s, perhaps after his fortunes fell.","why":"Merchants (tamkārum) were the traders and bankers of Babylonia.","people":[["ea-nasir","Ea-nasir"]]},{"n":6,"lv":6,"band":"specialists","name":"Soldiers on service land","loc":"Rēdûm","term":"bā'irum, ilkum (service)","lbl":"c. 3–8% (est.)","w":5.5,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/old-babylonia-6.jpg","you":"You hold a field, an orchard and a house from the king, and when he calls a campaign you must march; your land cannot be sold or taken for your debts, and if you are captured your son will take over your service.","kid":"Soldiers were given land by the king, and in return they had to fight whenever he called them.","rise":"Service land passed to a son who could do the service, so soldiering often ran in families; a man who failed to serve, or left his field, lost it to another who did.","why":"The king’s soldiers held land from the crown in return for service, the obligation called ilkum.","people":[]},{"n":7,"lv":7,"band":"commoners","name":"Free men and their families","loc":"Awīlum","term":"mār awīlim","lbl":"About 71% (the rest, est.)","w":70.8,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/old-babylonia-7.jpg","you":"You farm barley and dates on the family plot or on rented land; the law repays any injury to you in kind, but one flood or drought can put your family in debt.","kid":"Most free Babylonians were farmers who grew barley and dates in fields watered by canals.","rise":"A family that prospered could buy land, lend at interest or win a service field; one that fell into debt could lose its land or have to send a wife, son or daughter into debt service.","why":"The awīlum, literally “man”, was the free man of full standing in the Code of Hammurabi, and his household was the basic unit of Babylonian society.","people":[]},{"n":8,"lv":8,"band":"commoners","name":"Commoners","loc":"Muškēnum","term":"","lbl":"c. 10–25% (est.)","w":17.5,"firm":0,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/old-babylonia-8.jpg","you":"You are free and may own a field and even a slave, but the law counts you for less than an awīlum: whoever blinds you pays silver instead of losing his own eye.","kid":"The laws treated muškēnū as free people, but of lower rank than the full free men called awīlū.","rise":"How a family came to be counted among the muškēnū, or rose out of them, is not known.","why":"The muškēnum stood between the full free man and the slave, and who he was is still debated.","people":[]},{"n":9,"lv":9,"band":"enslaved","name":"Slaves","loc":"Wardum","term":"amtum (female slave)","lbl":"c. 2–6% (est.)","w":4,"firm":1,"est":1,"pic":"/images/societies/thumbs/old-babylonia-9.jpg","you":"You belong to another household, which can sell you, pledge you for its debts or give you away with a dowry; your hairlock marks you as a slave, and anyone who shelters you if you run away risks death.","kid":"Slaves were owned by other people and had to work without pay, though some were set free.","rise":"Owners could free slaves, often by adopting them, as nadītu at Sippar did with the women they made their heirs; family members bound for debt went free after three years or when a king proclaimed a release.","why":"Slaves (wardum, amtum) were property: they could be sold, pledged for debt, given as part of a dowry and inherited, and they wore a distinctive hairlock (abbuttum) that no barber might shave off without the owner’s…","people":[]}]}</scri